{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**The problem.** You're given an undirected graph with `n` vertices, numbered `0` to `n - 1`, and a list of `edges`. No edge is listed twice and no vertex is joined to itself. Return `true` if the graph contains a **cycle** — a route that leaves a vertex and comes back to it without using any edge twice."
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/d99e1f87-7cb0-4945-9161-34421db2e052.svg",
      "alt": "Left: a tree with four vertices; it has no cycle, because there is exactly one route between any two vertices. Right: five vertices where 1, 2 and 3 form a triangle, drawn in red. A walk from 0 reaches 1, 2, then 3, and from 3 sees 1 again — 1 is visited and is not the vertex 3 came from, so there is a cycle.",
      "width": 728,
      "height": 419,
      "scene": {
