    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "n = 5, edges = [[0,1],[1,2],[2,3],[3,1],[3,4]]   ->  true    (1 - 2 - 3 - 1)\nn = 4, edges = [[0,1],[0,2],[1,3]]               ->  false   (a tree)\nn = 6, edges = [[0,1],[1,2],[3,4],[4,5],[5,3]]   ->  true    (the loop is in the second piece)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The pattern: cycles and two-colouring"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "BFS and DFS answer *what can I reach?* Remember **one extra fact** per vertex and the same walk answers questions about the graph's shape. Remember where each vertex was reached **from**, and you can spot loops. Remember a **colour**, and you can split the vertices into two teams (next lesson). Either way it's still one walk: O(V + E)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Here's the key fact. In a graph with no cycle — a tree, or several trees — there is **exactly one route** between any two vertices. A cycle means some pair has **two** routes. During a DFS, a second route shows up as an edge to a vertex that's already visited."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "One catch: in an undirected graph, the edge you just came along also leads to a visited vertex — your parent. That isn't a second route, it's the same edge seen from the other end. So each call remembers its **parent** and ignores it. Any *other* visited neighbour is a cycle."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Brute force: test every edge"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "An edge lies on a cycle exactly when its two ends are **still connected without it**. So remove each edge in turn and search from one end for the other."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function hasCycle(n, edges) {\n  // An edge closes a cycle if its two ends are still connected without it.\n  for (let skip = 0; skip < edges.length; skip++) {\n    const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);\n    edges.forEach(([u, v], i) => {\n      if (i !== skip) {\n        adj[u].push(v);\n        adj[v].push(u);\n      }\n    });\n    const [from, to] = edges[skip];\n    const seen = new Array(n).fill(false);\n    const stack = [from];\n    seen[from] = true;\n    while (stack.length > 0) {\n      const u = stack.pop();\n      if (u === to) return true;          // reachable another way: this edge closes a cycle\n      for (const v of adj[u]) {\n        if (!seen[v]) {\n          seen[v] = true;\n          stack.push(v);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def has_cycle(n, edges):\n    # An edge closes a cycle if its two ends are still connected without it.\n    for skip in range(len(edges)):\n        adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n        for i, (u, v) in enumerate(edges):\n            if i != skip:\n                adj[u].append(v)\n                adj[v].append(u)\n        start, target = edges[skip]\n        seen = [False] * n\n        seen[start] = True\n        stack = [start]\n        while stack:\n            u = stack.pop()\n            if u == target:\n                return True               # reachable another way: this edge closes a cycle\n            for v in adj[u]:\n                if not seen[v]:\n                    seen[v] = True\n                    stack.append(v)\n    return False"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "boolean hasCycle(int n, int[][] edges) {\n    // An edge closes a cycle if its two ends are still connected without it.\n    for (int skip = 0; skip < edges.length; skip++) {\n        List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());\n        for (int i = 0; i < edges.length; i++) {\n            if (i == skip) continue;\n            adj.get(edges[i][0]).add(edges[i][1]);\n            adj.get(edges[i][1]).add(edges[i][0]);\n        }\n        int from = edges[skip][0], to = edges[skip][1];\n        boolean[] seen = new boolean[n];\n        seen[from] = true;\n        ArrayDeque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        stack.push(from);\n        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {\n            int u = stack.pop();\n            if (u == to) return true;            // reachable another way: this edge closes a cycle\n            for (int v : adj.get(u)) {\n                if (!seen[v]) {\n                    seen[v] = true;\n                    stack.push(v);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "bool hasCycle(int n, vector<vector<int>>& edges) {\n    // An edge closes a cycle if its two ends are still connected without it.\n    for (size_t skip = 0; skip < edges.size(); skip++) {\n        vector<vector<int>> adj(n);\n        for (size_t i = 0; i < edges.size(); i++) {\n            if (i == skip) continue;\n            adj[edges[i][0]].push_back(edges[i][1]);\n            adj[edges[i][1]].push_back(edges[i][0]);\n        }\n        int from = edges[skip][0], to = edges[skip][1];\n        vector<bool> seen(n, false);\n        seen[from] = true;\n        vector<int> stack = {from};\n        while (!stack.empty()) {\n            int u = stack.back(); stack.pop_back();\n            if (u == to) return true;            // reachable another way: this edge closes a cycle\n            for (int v : adj[u]) {\n                if (!seen[v]) {\n                    seen[v] = true;\n                    stack.push_back(v);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(E · (V + E)), space O(V + E).** Each of the E edges rebuilds the graph and runs a whole search. It works, but it repeats almost the same search E times."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. DFS remembering the parent"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Walk each piece of the graph once. Every call `dfs(u, parent)` marks `u` visited and looks at its neighbours: skip the parent, return `true` for any other visited neighbour, and otherwise recurse. The outer loop starts a new walk from every vertex not yet visited, because the cycle might be in a piece the first walk never reaches."
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "detect-cycle",
      "caption": "Each call remembers where it came from; a visited neighbour that isn't the parent closes a loop. Try the tree too."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function hasCycle(n, edges) {\n  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);\n  for (const [u, v] of edges) {\n    adj[u].push(v);\n    adj[v].push(u);\n  }\n  const visited = new Array(n).fill(false);\n  const dfs = (u, parent) => {\n    visited[u] = true;\n    for (const v of adj[u]) {\n      if (v === parent) continue;           // the edge we just came along: not a cycle\n      if (visited[v]) return true;          // reached again by another route: a cycle\n      if (dfs(v, u)) return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n  };\n  for (let s = 0; s < n; s++) {\n    if (!visited[s] && dfs(s, -1)) return true;   // check every piece of the graph\n  }\n  return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def has_cycle(n, edges):\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    for u, v in edges:\n        adj[u].append(v)\n        adj[v].append(u)\n    visited = [False] * n\n\n    def dfs(u, parent):\n        visited[u] = True\n        for v in adj[u]:\n            if v == parent:\n                continue                  # the edge we just came along: not a cycle\n            if visited[v]:\n                return True               # reached again by another route: a cycle\n            if dfs(v, u):\n                return True\n        return False\n\n    # check every piece of the graph\n    return any(not visited[s] and dfs(s, -1) for s in range(n))"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "boolean hasCycle(int n, int[][] edges) {\n    List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());\n    for (int[] e : edges) {\n        adj.get(e[0]).add(e[1]);\n        adj.get(e[1]).add(e[0]);\n    }\n    boolean[] visited = new boolean[n];\n    for (int s = 0; s < n; s++) {\n        if (!visited[s] && dfs(adj, visited, s, -1)) return true;   // check every piece of the graph\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n\nboolean dfs(List<List<Integer>> adj, boolean[] visited, int u, int parent) {\n    visited[u] = true;\n    for (int v : adj.get(u)) {\n        if (v == parent) continue;               // the edge we just came along: not a cycle\n        if (visited[v]) return true;             // reached again by another route: a cycle\n        if (dfs(adj, visited, v, u)) return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "bool dfs(vector<vector<int>>& adj, vector<bool>& visited, int u, int parent) {\n    visited[u] = true;\n    for (int v : adj[u]) {\n        if (v == parent) continue;               // the edge we just came along: not a cycle\n        if (visited[v]) return true;             // reached again by another route: a cycle\n        if (dfs(adj, visited, v, u)) return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n\nbool hasCycle(int n, vector<vector<int>>& edges) {\n    vector<vector<int>> adj(n);\n    for (auto& e : edges) {\n        adj[e[0]].push_back(e[1]);\n        adj[e[1]].push_back(e[0]);\n    }\n    vector<bool> visited(n, false);\n    for (int s = 0; s < n; s++) {\n        if (!visited[s] && dfs(adj, visited, s, -1)) return true;   // check every piece of the graph\n    }\n    return false;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(V + E), space O(V + E).** Every vertex is visited once and every edge is looked at from both ends. The adjacency list takes O(V + E); the recursion can be V calls deep."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Brute force: test every edge",
          "time": "O(E · (V + E))",
          "timeWhy": "Each of the **E** edges rebuilds the graph and runs a **whole search**.",
          "space": "O(V + E)",
          "spaceWhy": "One graph and one search at a time."
        },
        {
          "approach": "DFS remembering the parent",
          "time": "O(V + E)",
          "timeWhy": "Every vertex is visited **once**, every edge looked at from both ends.",
          "space": "O(V + E)",
          "spaceWhy": "The adjacency list, plus recursion up to **V** deep."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "A shortcut worth knowing: a graph with no cycle has **at most V - 1 edges**. So if `edges.length >= n`, the answer is `true` before you even search."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**No edges** — no cycle.",
        "**A cycle in a piece the first walk never reaches** — this is why the outer loop tries every start vertex.",
        "**One big cycle through every vertex** — the second route is only found at the very last edge.",
        "**The same edge listed twice** — `u - v - u` is then a real loop, but skipping the parent *vertex* hides it. If duplicates can appear, skip the parent *edge* (by its index) instead."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Graph Valid Tree** — a tree is connected with no cycle: check there are exactly `n - 1` edges and that one walk reaches every vertex.",
        "**Redundant Connection** — return the edge that closes the cycle. Union-Find (Pattern 7) spots it the moment it's added.",
        "**Cycle in a directed graph** — the parent trick doesn't apply; a DFS needs three states instead (unvisited, on the current path, finished). That's Course Schedule, Pattern 5."
      ]
    }
  ],
